WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.240
What's up? Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners episode 32 for May 8th, 2026.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.820
Today's AI news is pretty exciting, and I promise no jargon. Let's go.

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:18.280
So imagine you are in a long group chat trying to plan a school project with friends while also

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:24.400
texting someone else about weekend plans at the same time. Bits from one conversation start leaking

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:30.840
into the other, and suddenly details get mixed up or forgotten. That is basically what happens when

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:38.200
people try to use AI helpers for real work like homework or small side jobs. The AI keeps track of

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:44.640
everything in one running conversation, but after many steps it can mix details or even invent facts

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:51.120
that sound believable. Think of it like a busy waiter who is juggling three tables at once and starts

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:57.100
confusing orders because the notepad is getting too full. This is not because the AI is dumb.

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:03.200
It is because it has no separate memory folders for each task, so everything blends together.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:10.320
The result can waste time or create awkward moments, like handing in an essay with a made up website

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:16.580
reference. Here is why this matters for you. More students and beginners are turning to these

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:23.320
tools for creative projects or quick help. The fixes often not a smarter model, but adding simple

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:29.880
ways for a human to check the work first, like treating the output as a rough draft. You can test

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:36.840
this yourself right now. Open any chatbot, give it two different tasks in the same chat, then ask

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:44.100
it to combine them. Watch which details it keeps and which ones it mixes up. That quick experiment shows

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:51.840
the memory limits in action. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, where we slow down and

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:58.080
really see how something works under the hood. Think of riding your bike down the street. Your speed

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:04.300
changes in tiny ways even if you are not touching the brakes. First you notice you are going faster.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:10.460
That is velocity. Then you feel yourself speeding up or slowing down. That is acceleration.

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:18.260
Then comes the sudden alert when you hit the brakes. That is jerk. It is like the change of the change.

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:24.880
Now imagine those changes themselves start changing again. The fourth level is called snap.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:32.880
The fifth is crackle. The sixth is pop. It sounds like breakfast cereal, but it is really just a

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:39.840
way to track how motion gets more complicated the longer you follow it. And AI builds a little world

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:46.420
where you drag sliders and watch a dot move with all these layers at once. It does not understand

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:53.640
physics like a scientist. It learned patterns from millions of moving object examples and guesses

00:02:53.640 --> 00:03:00.340
what should happen next. So next time someone mentions higher order derivatives, you can picture the

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:07.500
bike wobble getting wilder as speed increases. That is the idea. And that is basically how these layered

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:16.600
motion rules work. Not so scary once you see the bike example. Now let's look at some cool stuff you

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:23.420
can actually try today. There is a simple web page that turns those same motion ideas into a toy you

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:31.160
can play with on your laptop or phone. You drag sliders for velocity, acceleration, jerk, snap, crackle,

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:37.600
and pop while watching a dot move across the screen. It feels like testing game character movement

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:44.560
instead of doing textbook math. Go to the page set only snap high while everything else stays low.

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:51.820
Then switch to making pop high. Notice how the dots motion suddenly feels different almost like it

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:58.420
has its own personality. That is the experiment. Another interesting update comes from the dating app

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:04.380
Bumble. They are building an AI helper that suggests matches instead of making you swipe through

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:09.960
hundreds of profiles. The AI would learn what you usually like and bring those people forward,

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:16.380
almost like a friend who knows your type and points them out. You cannot try the new feature yet,

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:22.400
but you can open the app now and think about which past likes or dislikes you would want the AI to notice.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:29.980
That quick reflection shows how the tool might save time while also raising questions about whether it

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:38.560
narrows your options too much. Here are a couple of quick bits to wrap up the news. Meta is working on an AI

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:44.400
that could browse Instagram and actually buy things for you. Like a personal shopper living inside

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:50.820
the apps you already use. It would handle browsing and checkout so you don't have to, though it is still

00:04:50.820 --> 00:04:58.380
in early testing. In other news, leaders from the US and China are exploring formal talks about how to

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:05.580
handle powerful AI systems. It is still early, but it shows governments treating AI like something that

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:11.760
needs shared international rules. And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go, huh,

00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:19.040
that's cool. Go play with it. Curiosity is how every expert started. See you tomorrow. And if you'd

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:24.700
rather watch, then listen, find us on YouTube at Nerra Network, links in the show notes.

